    "updated_at": "2026-09-29T15:36:48Z",
    "body": "It seems as though my initial assessment wasn't quite right as far as what the major implementations do.\n\nChromium is designed in a way that would mean that it would not seek back to the beginning when seeking to the end while paused. This is because it [skips TimeChanged() if it is paused](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/platform/media/web_media_player_impl.cc;l=2118-2120;drc=4fc13c5ad1a034be2e6528404cc569b51c7b9299), and that is the function that would loop the media. However, there is also a fast path in the seek algorithm that skips the pipeline seek if it is paused at the seek target already. That fast path [runs OnBufferingStateChange() with a forced time change](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/platform/media/web_media_player_impl.cc;l=1173-1190;drc=4fc13c5ad1a034be2e6528404cc569b51c7b9299), which [calls TimeChanged()](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/platform/media/web_media_player_impl.cc;l=2407-2410;drc=4fc13c5ad1a034be2e6528404cc569b51c7b9299), so if the player is paused at the end and then seeks to the end, it _does_ jump to the beginning.\n\nFirefox simply does not ever jump to the beginning normally. However, this is a quirk of the seamless looping feature, which may not be intentional. When disabling that, it _does_ jump to the beginning.\n\nWebKit does jump to the beginning in all cases, as far as I can tell.",
